1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,574
On the morning of the third day, a Greek army of thousands picks up its shields and walks away.

2
00:00:06,074 --> 00:00:08,326
It leaves a few hundred men behind.

3
00:00:08,826 --> 00:00:11,104
Their king has ordered the rest to go.

4
00:00:11,454 --> 00:00:14,084
He stays, and so do the men who came with him.

5
00:00:14,584 --> 00:00:18,998
Behind them, high on a mountain, Persian soldiers have found a path nobody guarded.

6
00:00:19,498 --> 00:00:21,855
By midday the pass will be surrounded.

7
00:00:22,355 --> 00:00:26,261
For two days, these Greeks have been the hardest thing in the world to move.

8
00:00:26,611 --> 00:00:28,863
By the end of the third, they are dead.

9
00:00:29,363 --> 00:00:33,491
So how does a small force stop the largest army anyone has ever seen?

10
00:00:33,991 --> 00:00:37,259
And how does it lose, after all that, to a goat track?

11
00:00:37,759 --> 00:00:41,495
Most people will tell you it was courage, and then it was a traitor.

12
00:00:41,845 --> 00:00:42,965
Both are true.

13
00:00:43,315 --> 00:00:45,112
Neither is the real answer.

14
00:00:45,612 --> 00:00:47,630
The real answer is geometry.

15
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:50,362
And a plan with exactly one weak point.

16
00:00:50,862 --> 00:00:52,490
Let's go back to the start.

17
00:00:52,990 --> 00:00:55,463
It is the summer of 480 BC.

18
00:00:55,813 --> 00:00:59,029
Xerxes, king of Persia, is marching on Greece.

19
00:00:59,529 --> 00:01:03,162
His father tried this ten years earlier and lost at Marathon.

20
00:01:03,662 --> 00:01:07,242
Xerxes means to finish the job, and he has brought everything.

21
00:01:07,592 --> 00:01:11,980
Soldiers from every corner of an empire that stretches from Egypt to India.

22
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:13,730
A fleet to match.

23
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:18,715
A bridge of boats across the Hellespont, because he did not want to wait for a ferry.

24
00:01:19,215 --> 00:01:21,780
He has also spent years on preparation.

25
00:01:22,130 --> 00:01:27,937
Herodotus says food was stockpiled along the whole route, in depots built long before the army arrived.

26
00:01:28,287 --> 00:01:35,188
A canal was dug through the headland at Mount Athos so the fleet would not have to sail around a cape where a storm had wrecked his father's ships.

27
00:01:35,688 --> 00:01:37,094
This is not a raid.

28
00:01:37,444 --> 00:01:39,319
It is a state moving house.

29
00:01:39,819 --> 00:01:40,938
How many men?

30
00:01:41,288 --> 00:01:43,996
Herodotus says more than two and a half million.

31
00:01:44,496 --> 00:01:48,181
Herodotus was a wonderful storyteller and a poor accountant.

32
00:01:48,531 --> 00:01:53,140
Modern historians say somewhere between one hundred thousand and a few hundred thousand.

33
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:57,377
Even the low end is the largest force the Greek world has ever faced.

34
00:01:57,877 --> 00:01:59,921
And here is the part that matters.

35
00:02:00,271 --> 00:02:03,930
An army that size has a problem that has nothing to do with fighting.

36
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:06,714
It has to eat and drink every single day.

37
00:02:07,064 --> 00:02:10,541
Herodotus claims some rivers were drunk dry as it passed.

38
00:02:11,041 --> 00:02:18,332
That is an exaggeration, but it points at something true: a force this large cannot sit still for long, and it cannot spread out too thin.

39
00:02:18,682 --> 00:02:22,549
It has to keep moving along roads, and it has to keep winning quickly.

40
00:02:23,049 --> 00:02:25,523
The Greeks cannot beat it on open ground.

41
00:02:25,873 --> 00:02:27,057
They know that.

42
00:02:27,557 --> 00:02:30,695
So they look for a place where open ground does not exist.

43
00:02:31,195 --> 00:02:33,487
The Greek plan is not just one battle.

44
00:02:33,837 --> 00:02:35,842
It is two, at the same time.

45
00:02:36,342 --> 00:02:41,354
On land, they will block the road south at a narrow pass called Thermopylae, the Hot Gates.

46
00:02:41,704 --> 00:02:51,808
At sea, a few miles away off the northern tip of the island of Euboea, a Greek fleet, with the Athenians providing most of the ships, will fight the Persian navy at a place called Artemisium.

47
00:02:52,308 --> 00:02:54,483
The two positions protect each other.

48
00:02:54,833 --> 00:03:00,835
The Persian fleet cannot simply sail past and land troops behind the pass, because the Greek ships are in the way.

49
00:03:01,185 --> 00:03:05,156
The Persian army cannot simply march through, because the pass is blocked.

50
00:03:05,506 --> 00:03:08,449
If either front breaks, the other has to fall back.

51
00:03:08,949 --> 00:03:10,237
Remember that.

52
00:03:10,587 --> 00:03:12,293
It will matter at the end.

53
00:03:12,793 --> 00:03:15,527
Here the mountains come down almost to the sea.

54
00:03:15,877 --> 00:03:20,590
In places, the road between cliff and water is barely wide enough for a single cart.

55
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:23,050
Herodotus says exactly that.

56
00:03:23,550 --> 00:03:29,747
One thing to know about the ground today: the sea has long since retreated, and the pass is now a wide, flat plain.

57
00:03:30,097 --> 00:03:33,092
If you visit, you will not see the place the Greeks saw.

58
00:03:33,592 --> 00:03:41,196
The Greek coastline was far closer to the cliff, and the road squeezed through between them, past hot springs, which is how the place got its name.

59
00:03:41,696 --> 00:03:43,427
Now look at what that does.

60
00:03:43,777 --> 00:03:51,928
Imagine the Greek line packed shoulder to shoulder in the narrowest part, and say, for the sake of the picture, that each man takes up about a meter of frontage.

61
00:03:52,428 --> 00:03:57,780
In a gap of fifteen or twenty meters, that means perhaps fifteen or twenty men in the front rank.

62
00:03:58,130 --> 00:04:01,450
Behind them, more ranks, each one ready to step forward.

63
00:04:01,950 --> 00:04:03,291
Now the Persians.

64
00:04:03,641 --> 00:04:06,323
A hundred thousand men can march toward that line.

65
00:04:06,673 --> 00:04:10,748
But only fifteen or twenty of them can actually fight at any given moment.

66
00:04:11,098 --> 00:04:14,717
The rest are standing behind them, watching, with nothing to do.

67
00:04:15,067 --> 00:04:16,578
They cannot go around.

68
00:04:16,928 --> 00:04:18,868
They cannot go over the mountain.

69
00:04:19,218 --> 00:04:21,066
They have to come through the door.

70
00:04:21,566 --> 00:04:23,168
That is the whole trick.

71
00:04:23,518 --> 00:04:25,991
The pass does not make the Greeks stronger.

72
00:04:26,341 --> 00:04:28,216
It makes the Persians smaller.

73
00:04:28,566 --> 00:04:34,712
A hundred thousand men, squeezed through a space the width of a cart, are only a few dozen men at the point of the spear.

74
00:04:35,212 --> 00:04:38,805
This is where Leonidas, king of Sparta, takes his stand.

75
00:04:39,155 --> 00:04:44,559
He brings three hundred Spartan soldiers, chosen for the job, and every one of them has a living son.

76
00:04:44,909 --> 00:04:47,552
Sparta does not expect them to come back.

77
00:04:47,902 --> 00:04:50,558
It makes sure their family names will survive them.

78
00:04:51,058 --> 00:04:53,844
Around those three hundred gather thousands more.

79
00:04:54,194 --> 00:04:58,816
Men from Tegea and Corinth, Arcadia, Thespiae, Phocis and Locris.

80
00:04:59,166 --> 00:05:01,992
Herodotus counts about seven thousand in all.

81
00:05:02,342 --> 00:05:07,459
The Spartans are the most famous of them, and they are fewer than one in twenty of the men in the pass.

82
00:05:07,959 --> 00:05:10,094
Why only three hundred Spartans?

83
00:05:10,444 --> 00:05:21,225
Because Sparta was in the middle of a religious festival, and Herodotus says the Spartans sent Leonidas and his bodyguard as an advance force, planning to send the rest of the army after the festival was over.

84
00:05:21,725 --> 00:05:24,068
It was a dangerous way to fight a war.

85
00:05:24,418 --> 00:05:30,889
It tells you something about how seriously Sparta took the threat, and how little it expected the first battle to be decisive.

86
00:05:31,389 --> 00:05:34,280
They repair an old wall across the narrowest point.

87
00:05:34,630 --> 00:05:35,788
Then they wait.

88
00:05:36,288 --> 00:05:39,726
Xerxes arrives, camps, and does nothing for four days.

89
00:05:40,226 --> 00:05:43,285
Herodotus says he was waiting for the Greeks to leave.

90
00:05:43,635 --> 00:05:44,989
That may be true.

91
00:05:45,339 --> 00:05:52,058
A Persian king who has been told that the Greeks are weak, and who has brought an army that size, might well expect his enemy to run.

92
00:05:52,408 --> 00:05:54,595
But look at it from his side as well.

93
00:05:55,095 --> 00:05:57,699
His fleet has just lost ships to a storm.

94
00:05:58,049 --> 00:06:01,031
His scouts have told him that the Greek army is small.

95
00:06:01,381 --> 00:06:06,615
A few days of waiting costs him little, and if the Greeks leave without a fight, it costs him nothing.

96
00:06:07,115 --> 00:06:08,781
He sends a scout to look.

97
00:06:09,131 --> 00:06:11,162
The scout comes back confused.

98
00:06:11,512 --> 00:06:14,220
The Spartans in front of the wall are exercising.

99
00:06:14,570 --> 00:06:16,380
Others are combing their hair.

100
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:23,442
There is a Spartan exile in Xerxes' camp, a former king named Demaratus, and Xerxes asks him what this means.

101
00:06:23,792 --> 00:06:25,771
Demaratus answers plainly.

102
00:06:26,121 --> 00:06:28,959
Spartans comb their hair before a fight to the death.

103
00:06:29,309 --> 00:06:31,484
These men are getting ready to kill you.

104
00:06:31,984 --> 00:06:33,871
Xerxes does not believe him.

105
00:06:34,221 --> 00:06:36,825
On the fifth day, he sends his army in.

106
00:06:37,325 --> 00:06:43,640
Before the fighting starts, someone warns a Spartan named Dienekes that the Persian arrows are so many they will hide the sun.

107
00:06:43,990 --> 00:06:47,297
Dienekes replies, according to Herodotus, "Good.

108
00:06:47,647 --> 00:06:49,496
Then we will fight in the shade."

109
00:06:49,996 --> 00:06:51,272
Hold on to that.

110
00:06:51,622 --> 00:06:53,510
We will come back to the shade.

111
00:06:53,860 --> 00:06:56,529
And we will come back to whether he really said it.

112
00:06:57,029 --> 00:07:02,237
On the first day, Xerxes sends in the Medes, with orders to bring the Greeks to him alive.

113
00:07:02,737 --> 00:07:03,674
They try.

114
00:07:04,024 --> 00:07:06,016
The Greeks have longer spears.

115
00:07:06,366 --> 00:07:11,691
They have bronze armor and big round shields locked edge to edge, so there is no gap to reach through.

116
00:07:12,191 --> 00:07:21,280
A Greek hoplite in full kit carries something like twenty-five kilos of metal and wood, which is a terrible thing to wear in a race and a very good thing to stand behind in a corridor.

117
00:07:21,780 --> 00:07:25,712
The Persians have short spears, wicker shields, and plenty of courage.

118
00:07:26,062 --> 00:07:28,548
Courage is not much use in a corridor.

119
00:07:29,048 --> 00:07:31,040
Look at it as a matter of reach.

120
00:07:31,390 --> 00:07:35,505
A longer spear means a Greek can hit his enemy before the enemy can hit him.

121
00:07:35,855 --> 00:07:40,386
A big shield that overlaps with his neighbor's means there is no gap to hit back through.

122
00:07:40,886 --> 00:07:47,031
A Persian soldier coming up to that line does not get to use his speed, or his skill with a bow, or his numbers.

123
00:07:47,381 --> 00:07:48,800
He gets to be stabbed.

124
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:52,165
Wave after wave goes in and comes back in pieces.

125
00:07:52,515 --> 00:07:59,207
Herodotus says Xerxes, watching from a throne set up to see the battle, leaped to his feet three times in fear for his army.

126
00:07:59,707 --> 00:08:01,478
Then he sends in his best.

127
00:08:01,828 --> 00:08:05,109
The Immortals, ten thousand strong, the royal guard.

128
00:08:05,459 --> 00:08:06,813
They do no better.

129
00:08:07,313 --> 00:08:08,810
Day two is the same.

130
00:08:09,160 --> 00:08:14,590
The Greeks fight in rotation, each contingent taking its turn at the front and then pulling back to rest.

131
00:08:14,940 --> 00:08:17,127
This is more important than it sounds.

132
00:08:17,477 --> 00:08:20,159
A man fighting in a shield wall tires fast.

133
00:08:20,659 --> 00:08:28,094
Rotating the front rank means the Greek line is always fresh, and the Persians, who also rotate, are always walking into the same fresh line.

134
00:08:28,594 --> 00:08:33,671
Some ancient writers add that the Spartans pretended to run, then turned on the men chasing them.

135
00:08:34,021 --> 00:08:36,886
A corridor punishes anyone who rushes into it.

136
00:08:37,386 --> 00:08:42,789
Speaking of things that cost nothing, a like and a subscribe are free, and they keep this channel going.

137
00:08:43,139 --> 00:08:46,485
Ephialtes, as we are about to see, charged a fee.

138
00:08:46,985 --> 00:08:52,037
By the end of the second day, Xerxes has the biggest army in history and no way forward.

139
00:08:52,537 --> 00:08:55,258
And now ask the question a commander would ask.

140
00:08:55,608 --> 00:08:56,780
What does he do?

141
00:08:57,280 --> 00:08:58,491
He cannot wait.

142
00:08:58,841 --> 00:09:02,356
The Greeks can hold this road for as long as they have food and men.

143
00:09:02,706 --> 00:09:10,831
The Persians cannot hold their position forever, because they also have a fleet that is fighting at Artemisium, and an army that eats and drinks in vast amounts.

144
00:09:11,331 --> 00:09:15,055
Every day at the Hot Gates costs him something, and brings him nothing.

145
00:09:15,555 --> 00:09:17,208
He cannot force the front.

146
00:09:17,558 --> 00:09:19,589
Two days of attacks have shown that.

147
00:09:20,089 --> 00:09:23,670
So the only way to beat the Greeks is to take the pass away from them.

148
00:09:24,020 --> 00:09:27,288
And to do that, he needs to get men behind the Greek line.

149
00:09:27,788 --> 00:09:29,558
Which means he needs a path.

150
00:09:30,058 --> 00:09:32,870
And then a local man walks into the Persian camp.

151
00:09:33,370 --> 00:09:35,128
His name is Ephialtes.

152
00:09:35,478 --> 00:09:38,798
He comes from Trachis, the region right behind the pass.

153
00:09:39,148 --> 00:09:42,690
Herodotus does not describe him as a soldier or as a villain.

154
00:09:43,040 --> 00:09:47,193
He is a man who knows something, and who sees that somebody will pay for it.

155
00:09:47,693 --> 00:09:49,724
What he knows is the mountain track.

156
00:09:50,074 --> 00:09:55,269
A path winds over Mount Kallidromon, behind the Greek position, and comes down at their rear.

157
00:09:55,619 --> 00:09:57,364
It is called the Anopaia.

158
00:09:57,714 --> 00:10:02,466
It is on no Persian map, and it does not need to be once somebody has been paid to walk it.

159
00:10:02,966 --> 00:10:05,778
Herodotus says Ephialtes did it for money.

160
00:10:06,128 --> 00:10:09,279
He does not say how much, or whether it was ever paid.

161
00:10:09,779 --> 00:10:12,279
Now here is the part most tellings leave out.

162
00:10:12,629 --> 00:10:14,452
The Greeks knew about the path.

163
00:10:14,802 --> 00:10:18,825
Local people from the region had told them about it when they first arrived.

164
00:10:19,175 --> 00:10:23,237
Leonidas had posted a thousand men from Phocis on the track to guard it.

165
00:10:23,587 --> 00:10:26,751
And Herodotus says the Phocians volunteered for the job.

166
00:10:27,251 --> 00:10:29,191
So this was not carelessness.

167
00:10:29,541 --> 00:10:32,470
It was a decision, and a reasonable-sounding one.

168
00:10:32,820 --> 00:10:34,903
The path was long and difficult.

169
00:10:35,253 --> 00:10:38,079
It would take a large force many hours to climb it.

170
00:10:38,429 --> 00:10:44,848
A thousand local men who knew the mountain, in the dark, in an oak forest where every step makes noise, looked like enough.

171
00:10:45,348 --> 00:10:48,655
It was not enough, and the reason is not complicated.

172
00:10:49,005 --> 00:10:51,257
A thousand men can stop a patrol.

173
00:10:51,607 --> 00:10:57,805
They cannot stop ten thousand of the best soldiers in the Persian army, led by a commander who has been given a guide.

174
00:10:58,305 --> 00:11:02,771
That night, Hydarnes, commander of the Immortals, leads his men up the track.

175
00:11:03,121 --> 00:11:06,571
It runs through an oak forest, and the leaves crunch underfoot.

176
00:11:06,921 --> 00:11:09,785
The Phocians hear them long before they see anything.

177
00:11:10,285 --> 00:11:13,540
They grab their weapons and run to a hilltop to make a stand.

178
00:11:13,890 --> 00:11:16,898
Herodotus says they thought the Persians had come for them.

179
00:11:17,248 --> 00:11:19,058
The Persians do not bother.

180
00:11:19,408 --> 00:11:22,129
They fire some arrows, and then they keep walking.

181
00:11:22,629 --> 00:11:25,298
The Phocians have just saved the enemy a fight.

182
00:11:25,648 --> 00:11:29,267
And down in the pass, the Greeks are about to get the bad news.

183
00:11:29,767 --> 00:11:34,298
Step back for a moment, because this is the real answer to the question we started with.

184
00:11:34,798 --> 00:11:36,882
The Greek plan had one assumption.

185
00:11:37,232 --> 00:11:40,083
The plan said: the pass is the only way through.

186
00:11:40,433 --> 00:11:43,870
If that is true, a small force can hold it indefinitely.

187
00:11:44,370 --> 00:11:45,828
But it was not true.

188
00:11:46,178 --> 00:11:49,472
There was another way, narrow and slow and long, but a way.

189
00:11:49,822 --> 00:11:57,934
The whole defense depended on it staying closed, and it was defended by a small force that had to guard a long, dark mountain path against an army with a guide.

190
00:11:58,434 --> 00:12:00,426
Engineers have a name for this.

191
00:12:00,776 --> 00:12:02,560
A single point of failure.

192
00:12:02,910 --> 00:12:07,532
A system that works perfectly until one part fails, and then does not work at all.

193
00:12:08,032 --> 00:12:10,467
Notice what this means for Ephialtes.

194
00:12:10,817 --> 00:12:14,319
We have been taught to see him as the man who destroyed Thermopylae.

195
00:12:14,669 --> 00:12:20,033
But a plan that can be destroyed by one man walking into a camp was a plan with a hole in it already.

196
00:12:20,533 --> 00:12:22,994
Ephialtes did not create the weakness.

197
00:12:23,344 --> 00:12:25,388
He sold the Persians a map of it.

198
00:12:25,888 --> 00:12:28,258
That is not an excuse for what he did.

199
00:12:28,608 --> 00:12:30,287
But it changes the lesson.

200
00:12:30,637 --> 00:12:35,899
The lesson is not "beware of traitors." Every army has men who can be bought.

201
00:12:36,249 --> 00:12:39,843
The lesson is: do not build a plan where one of them is enough.

202
00:12:40,343 --> 00:12:42,960
At dawn, a scout runs in with the news.

203
00:12:43,310 --> 00:12:46,304
Soon men are coming down from the mountain to confirm it.

204
00:12:46,804 --> 00:12:48,640
The Greek commanders argue.

205
00:12:48,990 --> 00:12:50,292
Some want to stay.

206
00:12:50,642 --> 00:12:51,983
Some want to leave.

207
00:12:52,333 --> 00:12:56,057
In the end, Leonidas sends the allied contingents away.

208
00:12:56,407 --> 00:12:59,427
They still have an army worth saving and a war to fight.

209
00:12:59,927 --> 00:13:01,177
Why does he stay?

210
00:13:01,527 --> 00:13:03,519
Herodotus gives two reasons.

211
00:13:03,869 --> 00:13:09,207
The first is an oracle that told Sparta either the city would be destroyed or a Spartan king would die.

212
00:13:09,557 --> 00:13:11,133
The second is simpler.

213
00:13:11,483 --> 00:13:13,344
Spartans do not retreat.

214
00:13:13,694 --> 00:13:16,910
They are trained from boyhood to hold their ground or die on it.

215
00:13:17,410 --> 00:13:20,926
Historians are suspicious of the oracle, and with reason.

216
00:13:21,276 --> 00:13:24,869
Prophecies that fit the outcome tend to be written after the outcome.

217
00:13:25,219 --> 00:13:28,045
A cleaner military explanation exists.

218
00:13:28,395 --> 00:13:36,038
If the whole Greek army had tried to retreat in daylight with the Persian cavalry about to arrive, it could have been caught on open ground and destroyed.

219
00:13:36,538 --> 00:13:40,105
A rearguard that holds the pass long enough lets the rest slip away.

220
00:13:40,455 --> 00:13:43,541
Leonidas may not have been choosing to die for honor.

221
00:13:43,891 --> 00:13:47,133
He may have been choosing to die so that the others could live.

222
00:13:47,633 --> 00:13:50,015
Whatever the reason, the Spartans stay.

223
00:13:50,365 --> 00:13:56,107
So do seven hundred men from the small city of Thespiae, who refuse to leave a fight their allies are staying for.

224
00:13:56,457 --> 00:14:02,746
Four hundred Thebans stay too, though Herodotus says Leonidas kept them as hostages and that they surrendered at the end.

225
00:14:03,246 --> 00:14:07,569
That is about fourteen hundred men against an army of a hundred thousand or more.

226
00:14:08,069 --> 00:14:10,621
Leonidas does not wait to be surrounded.

227
00:14:10,971 --> 00:14:16,700
In the morning, he leads his men out of the narrow pass, onto the wider ground in front of it, and fights there.

228
00:14:17,200 --> 00:14:18,358
Not to win.

229
00:14:18,708 --> 00:14:23,161
To kill as many as they can, and to buy time for the rest of the army to get away.

230
00:14:23,661 --> 00:14:25,497
Think about what that means.

231
00:14:25,847 --> 00:14:31,107
Everything that made the pass work for the Greeks, the narrow front, is gone the moment they step out of it.

232
00:14:31,457 --> 00:14:34,829
They do it anyway, because the pass is no longer holding.

233
00:14:35,329 --> 00:14:36,800
It has been turned.

234
00:14:37,150 --> 00:14:42,359
The only thing left to do is to hurt the enemy as badly as possible, for as long as possible.

235
00:14:42,859 --> 00:14:45,371
They fight with spears until the spears break.

236
00:14:45,721 --> 00:14:46,880
Then swords.

237
00:14:47,230 --> 00:14:51,201
Leonidas falls, and the battle turns into a fight over his body.

238
00:14:51,551 --> 00:14:56,290
Herodotus says the Greeks drove the Persians back four times and finally dragged it away.

239
00:14:56,790 --> 00:14:59,108
Then the Immortals arrive from behind.

240
00:14:59,458 --> 00:15:02,986
The Greeks fall back to a small hill and make their last stand there.

241
00:15:03,336 --> 00:15:06,448
Men with no swords left fight with their hands and teeth.

242
00:15:06,798 --> 00:15:10,561
Persian archers and javelin throwers finish the job from a distance.

243
00:15:11,061 --> 00:15:12,363
Remember the shade?

244
00:15:12,713 --> 00:15:14,171
Dienekes was right.

245
00:15:14,521 --> 00:15:17,294
That day, the sky does go dark with arrows.

246
00:15:17,644 --> 00:15:20,443
Whether Dienekes ever said it, we do not know.

247
00:15:20,943 --> 00:15:28,951
The line comes from Herodotus, who was writing a generation later, and it sounds a lot like the kind of thing people say about a battle once they know how it ended.

248
00:15:29,451 --> 00:15:34,529
But archaeologists have found Persian arrowheads on the hill, so the arrows at least are real.

249
00:15:35,029 --> 00:15:39,404
Afterward, Xerxes has Leonidas's head cut off and fixed on a stake.

250
00:15:39,754 --> 00:15:45,691
Herodotus notes this was a strange thing for a Persian king to do, because Persians honored brave enemies.

251
00:15:46,041 --> 00:15:49,660
He takes it as a sign of how badly Thermopylae shook Xerxes.

252
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:54,158
Herodotus also tells a story that, if true, says even more.

253
00:15:54,508 --> 00:16:03,205
Xerxes, he says, buried most of his own dead and left only a thousand bodies lying out, so that visitors would see how many Greeks had died and how few Persians.

254
00:16:03,705 --> 00:16:06,713
Herodotus did not believe the trick fooled anyone.

255
00:16:07,063 --> 00:16:08,313
Neither should we.

256
00:16:08,663 --> 00:16:12,425
A man who hides his casualties is a man who knows what they look like.

257
00:16:12,925 --> 00:16:17,014
Here is where most tellings stop, with a poem and a moment of silence.

258
00:16:17,364 --> 00:16:19,239
But there is a harder question.

259
00:16:19,589 --> 00:16:21,138
Did Thermopylae work?

260
00:16:21,638 --> 00:16:24,085
Look at it as a military operation.

261
00:16:24,435 --> 00:16:26,675
The Greek plan was to hold the pass.

262
00:16:27,025 --> 00:16:28,782
They did not hold the pass.

263
00:16:29,132 --> 00:16:30,994
In that sense, it failed.

264
00:16:31,494 --> 00:16:33,564
Look at what it cost the Persians.

265
00:16:33,914 --> 00:16:34,982
A few days.

266
00:16:35,332 --> 00:16:38,235
A good number of dead, though nobody knows how many.

267
00:16:38,585 --> 00:16:46,970
And one more thing: the Greek fleet at Artemisium, which had fought the Persian navy to a bruising draw over the same three days, now heard that the pass had fallen.

268
00:16:47,470 --> 00:16:52,145
With the land route open, there was nothing left to protect, and the fleet withdrew south.

269
00:16:52,645 --> 00:16:55,379
So the Persians went on, and they burned Athens.

270
00:16:55,879 --> 00:16:59,876
If that were the end of the story, Thermopylae would be a brave failure.

271
00:17:00,226 --> 00:17:01,801
But it is not the end.

272
00:17:02,301 --> 00:17:06,181
The Greek fleet withdrew to the narrow waters off the island of Salamis.

273
00:17:06,531 --> 00:17:10,997
And there, a few weeks later, the same thing happened that had happened at the Hot Gates.

274
00:17:11,347 --> 00:17:18,079
The Persian fleet, far larger, was lured into a narrow channel, where its numbers were no use, and the Greeks destroyed it.

275
00:17:18,579 --> 00:17:21,782
Xerxes had watched the battle from a throne on the shore.

276
00:17:22,132 --> 00:17:25,595
He went home soon after, leaving part of his army behind.

277
00:17:25,945 --> 00:17:28,927
That army was beaten at Plataea the following year.

278
00:17:29,427 --> 00:17:30,546
Do you see it?

279
00:17:30,896 --> 00:17:33,031
Thermopylae did not win the war.

280
00:17:33,381 --> 00:17:36,259
But it taught the Greeks the shape of the war they could win.

281
00:17:36,609 --> 00:17:41,035
Not a battle on open ground, but a battle where the ground did the fighting for them.

282
00:17:41,535 --> 00:17:44,217
They tried it at the pass, and it nearly worked.

283
00:17:44,567 --> 00:17:47,184
They tried it again at Salamis, and it did.

284
00:17:47,684 --> 00:17:50,406
There was a second gift, harder to measure.

285
00:17:50,756 --> 00:17:54,935
Before Thermopylae, many Greeks believed the Persians could not be stopped.

286
00:17:55,285 --> 00:17:58,722
After it, they had seen a few thousand men do it for two days.

287
00:17:59,072 --> 00:18:02,471
That is the kind of thing an army carries into its next fight.

288
00:18:02,971 --> 00:18:04,390
So was it worth it?

289
00:18:04,740 --> 00:18:06,497
You can argue both ways.

290
00:18:06,847 --> 00:18:09,581
The men who died did not get to see Salamis.

291
00:18:09,931 --> 00:18:13,850
But the Greeks who did see it had been told, by those men, what to do.

292
00:18:14,350 --> 00:18:19,689
Before the end, the Spartans had sent three men away, two with an eye disease and one on a message.

293
00:18:20,039 --> 00:18:23,736
Herodotus says one of the sick men went back into the fight and died.

294
00:18:24,086 --> 00:18:27,458
The other two lived, and were treated as outcasts at home.

295
00:18:27,958 --> 00:18:36,825
One of them, Aristodemus, is said to have redeemed himself a year later at Plataea, fighting with a kind of fury that his fellow Spartans did not recognize as courage.

296
00:18:37,325 --> 00:18:41,492
Later, the Greeks put up a stone at the pass with a poem by Simonides:

297
00:18:41,992 --> 00:18:47,330
Go tell the Spartans, stranger passing by, that here, obedient to their laws, we lie.

298
00:18:47,830 --> 00:18:49,249
And Ephialtes?

299
00:18:49,599 --> 00:18:52,750
He fled when the news got out, with a price on his head.

300
00:18:53,100 --> 00:18:57,449
Herodotus says he was eventually killed, though over something else entirely.

301
00:18:57,799 --> 00:19:01,054
The Spartans paid the reward to the man who killed him anyway.

302
00:19:01,554 --> 00:19:04,600
Thermopylae is not really a story about bravery.

303
00:19:04,950 --> 00:19:07,164
The Persians had plenty of that too.

304
00:19:07,664 --> 00:19:11,335
It is a story about a place, and what a place can do to an army.

305
00:19:11,685 --> 00:19:15,110
A narrow road made three hundred as strong as three hundred thousand.

306
00:19:15,460 --> 00:19:18,350
Then a narrow path made them as weak as three hundred.

307
00:19:18,850 --> 00:19:21,636
The Greeks never lost the pass in a fair fight.

308
00:19:21,986 --> 00:19:25,163
They lost it when somebody showed the enemy a way around.

309
00:19:25,663 --> 00:19:28,566
If you take one thing from the Hot Gates, take this.

310
00:19:28,916 --> 00:19:31,651
Counting the men in a fight will often mislead you.

311
00:19:32,001 --> 00:19:34,266
Count how many of them can reach each other.

312
00:19:34,766 --> 00:19:37,070
And count the paths nobody is watching.
